
## DOCKER ##
> docker is a tool that simplifies the process of developing, package, and deploying application.
> docker allows you to build, package, and run applications inside containers.

## DOCKER IMAGES ##
> A  docker image is a file made up of multiple layers that contains the instruction to build and run  a docker container .
> it act as an executable package that includes everything needed to run an application code, runtime, libraries,enviroment variable and configurarion

*** how it works ***
 1. image define how containor should be created 
 2. specify which software components will run and how they are configured.
 3. once an image is run, it become a docker container

** relation to container **
    docker image :- blueprint (static and read)
     docker container :- running instance of that image (dynamic, executable)

## CONTAINERS ##
> Containers are lightweight piece of software that contains all the code, libraries, and dependencies that the application needs to run.
> Containers share the host operating system’s kernel but keep applications and their dependencies isolated. This makes containers faster to start, less resource-hungry, and portable across environments.

** notes :- 1. A container is created when a Docker Image is executed.
            2. It runs as an isolated process on the host machine but shares the host’s operating system kernel.
            3.  Multiple containers can run on the same system without interfering with each other.

** relation to image **
    docker image :- blueprint (static, read only)
    docker container :- live instance of that blueprint (dynamic, executable)


### DOCKER HUB ###
>> Docker Hub is a repository service and it is a cloud based service where people push their Docker Container Images and also pull the Docker Container Images from the Docker Hub anytime or anywhere via the internet.

> Repository Service: Serves as a global storage hub where users can push (upload) or pull (download) container images from anywhere via the internet.

> Image Management: Offers the flexibility to host images in public registries (accessible to everyone) or private registries (restricted to specific users or teams).

> Collaboration: Simplifies the DevOps workflow by allowing teams to find, reuse, and share standardized environments.
> Accessibility: An open-source, freely available tool compatible with all major operating systems.

>Resource Library: Hosts millions of "Official Images" for popular software like Nginx, Python, and Ubuntu, ensuring a verified starting point for projects.

#### DOCKER COMMANDS ####

>>> docker image <<< 

1. list image :- docker images

2. download image :- docker pull image-name

3. remove image :- docker rmi image-name/image-id

4. remove image forcefully :- docker rmi -f image-name/image-id

>>> docker container <<<<

1. create and start container :- docker run container-name

2. run container in background :- docker run -d container-name

3. run container interctively :- docker run -it ubuntu /bin/bash

4. give a container name :- docker run -d --name nginx-container nginx

5. Run in background + interactive terminal :- docker run -itd --name ubuntu-container ubuntu /bin/bash

>>> container management <<<

1. list running container :- docker ps

2. list all container :- docker ps -a

3. start a stopped container :- docker start nginx-container

4. stop a container :- docker stop nginx-container

5. restart container :- docker restart nginx-container

6. remove a container :- docker rm nginx-container

7. force remove container :- docker rm -f nginx-container


